1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,490
It's almost midnight, and the kitchen faucet

2
00:00:02,566 --> 00:00:03,924
won't stop dripping.

3
00:00:04,226 --> 00:00:06,716
Maya is up late with her AP Chem homework,

4
00:00:06,754 --> 00:00:08,452
and her little brother Theo is supposed

5
00:00:08,528 --> 00:00:09,328
to be asleep.

6
00:00:09,811 --> 00:00:12,113
Instead he's watching the drops.

7
00:00:12,377 --> 00:00:14,641
Then he asks her something she can't answer

8
00:00:14,754 --> 00:00:15,376
yet.

9
00:00:15,396 --> 00:00:17,660
How many tiny pieces of water are in just

10
00:00:17,886 --> 00:00:19,018
one drop?

11
00:00:19,471 --> 00:00:21,283
Water is made of molecules,

12
00:00:21,358 --> 00:00:23,396
one oxygen, drawn here in red,

13
00:00:23,584 --> 00:00:24,830
holding two hydrogens.

14
00:00:25,207 --> 00:00:26,867
They're way too small to see,

15
00:00:26,943 --> 00:00:28,754
so how could anybody count them?

16
00:00:29,056 --> 00:00:31,320
Stick around, and you'll give Theo a real

17
00:00:31,396 --> 00:00:32,196
number.

18
00:00:32,226 --> 00:00:34,113
On the way, you'll convert units without

19
00:00:34,188 --> 00:00:36,301
guessing, work with powers of ten,

20
00:00:36,377 --> 00:00:39,018
and write huge and tiny numbers in scientific

21
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:40,150
notation.

22
00:00:40,264 --> 00:00:41,962
This sounds like the boring part,

23
00:00:42,075 --> 00:00:44,754
but year after year, AP exam readers report

24
00:00:44,867 --> 00:00:47,698
the same kinds of slips: leaving off units,

25
00:00:47,773 --> 00:00:49,811
mixing up joules and kilojoules,

26
00:00:49,886 --> 00:00:52,226
forgetting to change Celsius to kelvin.

27
00:00:52,301 --> 00:00:54,037
The chemistry can be perfect,

28
00:00:54,075 --> 00:00:56,075
and the answer still comes out wrong.

29
00:00:59,575 --> 00:01:01,386
Say your lab partner tells you the sample

30
00:01:01,462 --> 00:01:03,273
is twelve point six.

31
00:01:03,349 --> 00:01:04,933
Twelve point six what?

32
00:01:05,122 --> 00:01:05,744
Grams?

33
00:01:05,764 --> 00:01:06,564
Milliliters?

34
00:01:06,594 --> 00:01:07,394
Degrees?

35
00:01:07,650 --> 00:01:09,688
Without a unit, it tells you nothing.

36
00:01:10,141 --> 00:01:13,160
Twelve point six grams, now it means something.

37
00:01:13,386 --> 00:01:15,952
So keep the unit on, every line.

38
00:01:16,254 --> 00:01:18,141
Chemistry uses the metric system,

39
00:01:18,216 --> 00:01:20,179
which puts a prefix in front of a unit to

40
00:01:20,292 --> 00:01:21,092
change its size.

41
00:01:21,462 --> 00:01:24,179
Kilo, written k, means a thousand,

42
00:01:24,254 --> 00:01:26,707
so a kilogram is a thousand grams.

43
00:01:26,971 --> 00:01:29,462
Milli goes the other way: a thousandth.

44
00:01:29,537 --> 00:01:31,424
That's why it takes a thousand milliliters

45
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:32,933
to make one liter.

46
00:01:33,009 --> 00:01:36,556
Micro is a millionth, and nano is a billionth.

47
00:01:36,632 --> 00:01:39,273
A small m in front of a unit means milli,

48
00:01:39,386 --> 00:01:41,613
but m on its own means meters.

49
00:01:41,688 --> 00:01:44,254
The AP equation sheet lists the prefixes,

50
00:01:44,367 --> 00:01:45,915
so you just have to use them the right way

51
00:01:45,990 --> 00:01:46,820
round.

52
00:01:46,933 --> 00:01:48,745
So how do you switch units?

53
00:01:49,009 --> 00:01:50,556
Think about money for a second.

54
00:01:50,745 --> 00:01:52,481
A dollar is a hundred cents,

55
00:01:52,594 --> 00:01:53,215
right?

56
00:01:53,235 --> 00:01:55,650
So what's a hundred cents divided by one

57
00:01:55,801 --> 00:01:56,601
dollar?

58
00:01:56,632 --> 00:01:57,462
It's...

59
00:01:57,575 --> 00:01:58,196
one.

60
00:01:58,216 --> 00:02:00,179
Same amount on top and bottom.

61
00:02:00,405 --> 00:02:02,594
A conversion factor is exactly that.

62
00:02:02,971 --> 00:02:05,311
A thousand meters over one kilometer is

63
00:02:05,462 --> 00:02:07,688
one, because those are the same length.

64
00:02:07,877 --> 00:02:09,839
And when you multiply something by one,

65
00:02:09,915 --> 00:02:11,349
the amount can't change.

66
00:02:11,650 --> 00:02:13,688
Only the number and the unit change,

67
00:02:13,764 --> 00:02:14,564
together.

68
00:02:14,669 --> 00:02:16,783
This is called dimensional analysis,

69
00:02:16,820 --> 00:02:18,820
and the whole trick is which way up you

70
00:02:18,896 --> 00:02:19,839
put the factor.

71
00:02:20,103 --> 00:02:22,669
Units cancel like numbers do: the same unit

72
00:02:22,745 --> 00:02:25,235
on the top and on the bottom divides out.

73
00:02:25,462 --> 00:02:27,726
So put the unit you have on the bottom,

74
00:02:27,764 --> 00:02:29,462
and the unit you want on top.

75
00:02:29,915 --> 00:02:32,179
A trail is two point four five kilometers

76
00:02:32,254 --> 00:02:32,913
long.

77
00:02:32,933 --> 00:02:35,424
Times a thousand meters over one kilometer,

78
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:37,839
the kilometers cancel, and you get two thousand

79
00:02:37,915 --> 00:02:39,575
four hundred fifty meters.

80
00:02:39,650 --> 00:02:41,084
Then check the size.

81
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:43,575
A meter is much smaller than a kilometer,

82
00:02:43,650 --> 00:02:45,160
so you should need more of them,

83
00:02:45,235 --> 00:02:46,141
and you do.

84
00:02:46,216 --> 00:02:48,330
If your number had gotten smaller instead,

85
00:02:48,405 --> 00:02:50,216
the factor was upside down.

86
00:02:50,292 --> 00:02:53,084
That quick check catches most unit mistakes.

87
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:55,047
When there's no single factor between two

88
00:02:55,122 --> 00:02:56,783
units, chain a few.

89
00:02:56,858 --> 00:02:58,896
Say you want four thousand two hundred fifty

90
00:02:58,971 --> 00:03:00,933
milligrams in kilograms.

91
00:03:01,084 --> 00:03:03,273
Times one gram over a thousand milligrams

92
00:03:03,424 --> 00:03:05,613
gives four point two five grams,

93
00:03:05,688 --> 00:03:08,028
then times one kilogram over a thousand

94
00:03:08,103 --> 00:03:08,903
grams.

95
00:03:08,933 --> 00:03:11,349
Each factor cancels what the last one left,

96
00:03:11,424 --> 00:03:13,688
and you get point zero zero four two five

97
00:03:13,839 --> 00:03:14,639
kilograms.

98
00:03:14,971 --> 00:03:16,933
And any time you see the word per,

99
00:03:17,009 --> 00:03:18,669
that's a conversion factor too.

100
00:03:18,896 --> 00:03:20,933
Density is mass per volume,

101
00:03:21,009 --> 00:03:23,575
and water's is about one gram per milliliter,

102
00:03:23,688 --> 00:03:26,292
so it turns a volume of water into a mass.

103
00:03:26,518 --> 00:03:28,103
Temperature is the odd one out,

104
00:03:28,179 --> 00:03:30,216
because you add instead of multiply.

105
00:03:30,292 --> 00:03:33,160
Kelvin, written K with no degree sign,

106
00:03:33,235 --> 00:03:35,575
starts at the coldest temperature possible,

107
00:03:35,650 --> 00:03:37,764
and it's Celsius plus two seventy three

108
00:03:37,915 --> 00:03:38,896
point one five.

109
00:03:39,469 --> 00:03:41,688
So twenty five degrees Celsius is about

110
00:03:41,764 --> 00:03:44,028
two ninety eight kelvin, and forgetting

111
00:03:44,141 --> 00:03:46,632
that wrecks a lot of exam answers.

112
00:03:46,971 --> 00:03:49,537
Back in the kitchen, Maya starts by measuring.

113
00:03:49,613 --> 00:03:51,575
She holds a medicine cup under the faucet

114
00:03:51,650 --> 00:03:53,009
and counts drops.

115
00:03:53,084 --> 00:03:55,650
A hundred drops fill it to thirty milliliters,

116
00:03:55,726 --> 00:03:58,066
so one drop is thirty milliliters over a

117
00:03:58,103 --> 00:04:00,481
hundred, point three milliliters.

118
00:04:00,518 --> 00:04:02,367
And her phone timer says it drips about

119
00:04:02,481 --> 00:04:03,575
once a second.

120
00:04:03,726 --> 00:04:05,688
So how much water is that leak wasting in

121
00:04:05,764 --> 00:04:06,594
a day?

122
00:04:06,669 --> 00:04:09,386
One drop per second, times point three milliliters

123
00:04:09,462 --> 00:04:10,367
per drop.

124
00:04:10,481 --> 00:04:12,330
Then sixty seconds in a minute...

125
00:04:12,443 --> 00:04:13,915
sixty minutes in an hour...

126
00:04:14,028 --> 00:04:15,915
and twenty four hours in a day.

127
00:04:16,179 --> 00:04:18,292
Everything cancels except milliliters per

128
00:04:18,443 --> 00:04:20,933
day, and she gets about twenty six thousand

129
00:04:21,009 --> 00:04:22,367
milliliters a day.

130
00:04:22,443 --> 00:04:25,084
Twenty six liters, more than a bucket,

131
00:04:25,160 --> 00:04:26,896
from one little drip.

132
00:04:27,349 --> 00:04:28,149
Your turn.

133
00:04:28,330 --> 00:04:30,896
A car is moving at thirty meters per second.

134
00:04:31,273 --> 00:04:33,613
How fast is that in kilometers per hour?

135
00:04:33,839 --> 00:04:35,801
There are three thousand six hundred seconds

136
00:04:35,877 --> 00:04:36,677
in an hour.

137
00:04:36,707 --> 00:04:38,518
That's sixty times sixty.

138
00:04:38,632 --> 00:04:40,783
And there are a thousand meters in a kilometer.

139
00:04:47,635 --> 00:04:49,447
Start with thirty meters per second.

140
00:04:49,749 --> 00:04:51,635
Times three thousand six hundred seconds

141
00:04:51,711 --> 00:04:54,352
per hour, times one kilometer over a thousand

142
00:04:54,428 --> 00:04:55,228
meters.

143
00:04:55,333 --> 00:04:57,296
Seconds cancel, meters cancel,

144
00:04:57,371 --> 00:04:58,201
and you get...

145
00:04:58,277 --> 00:05:00,503
one hundred eight kilometers per hour.

146
00:05:00,767 --> 00:05:02,730
Did you get a tiny number instead?

147
00:05:02,956 --> 00:05:04,843
A lot of people divide by three thousand

148
00:05:04,918 --> 00:05:05,824
six hundred.

149
00:05:05,899 --> 00:05:07,069
But think about it.

150
00:05:07,107 --> 00:05:09,598
In a whole hour, a car goes way farther

151
00:05:09,673 --> 00:05:12,013
than in one second, so the number has to

152
00:05:12,088 --> 00:05:12,888
grow.

153
00:05:16,116 --> 00:05:18,532
Chemistry's numbers get ridiculously big

154
00:05:18,683 --> 00:05:21,475
and small, so we use exponents.

155
00:05:21,701 --> 00:05:24,569
In ten to the fourth, the ten is the base,

156
00:05:24,607 --> 00:05:26,833
and that little raised four is the exponent.

157
00:05:27,211 --> 00:05:29,173
It tells you how many tens you multiply

158
00:05:29,249 --> 00:05:30,049
together.

159
00:05:30,154 --> 00:05:33,022
Ten times ten times ten times ten is ten

160
00:05:33,173 --> 00:05:34,003
thousand.

161
00:05:34,079 --> 00:05:37,173
So ten to a positive power is a one followed

162
00:05:37,286 --> 00:05:38,607
by that many zeros.

163
00:05:38,833 --> 00:05:40,871
Now, what happens when you multiply two

164
00:05:40,909 --> 00:05:41,709
of them?

165
00:05:41,777 --> 00:05:44,494
Ten squared, which means ten to the second,

166
00:05:44,645 --> 00:05:47,098
times ten cubed, ten to the third.

167
00:05:47,362 --> 00:05:50,381
Write it out: two tens, then three tens.

168
00:05:50,532 --> 00:05:52,267
That's five tens in a row,

169
00:05:52,343 --> 00:05:54,003
so it's ten to the fifth.

170
00:05:54,230 --> 00:05:56,909
When you multiply, you add the exponents.

171
00:05:57,173 --> 00:05:58,607
Dividing works the other way.

172
00:05:59,060 --> 00:06:01,928
Ten to the fifth over ten squared: two tens

173
00:06:02,003 --> 00:06:04,041
on the bottom cancel two on top,

174
00:06:04,116 --> 00:06:06,833
so you subtract, and get ten cubed.

175
00:06:06,909 --> 00:06:09,324
Now take ten squared, and cube the whole

176
00:06:09,475 --> 00:06:10,059
thing.

177
00:06:10,079 --> 00:06:11,664
That's what the parentheses mean.

178
00:06:11,739 --> 00:06:13,928
It's ten squared three times over,

179
00:06:14,003 --> 00:06:17,022
which is six tens, so ten to the sixth.

180
00:06:17,098 --> 00:06:19,362
A power of a power multiplies.

181
00:06:19,739 --> 00:06:22,003
Oh, and these rules only work when the base

182
00:06:22,079 --> 00:06:22,879
is the same.

183
00:06:23,060 --> 00:06:25,852
Ten squared times two cubed isn't five of

184
00:06:25,928 --> 00:06:26,728
anything.

185
00:06:26,909 --> 00:06:28,947
Now watch what happens if we keep dividing.

186
00:06:29,399 --> 00:06:32,720
Ten cubed over ten cubed is ten to the zero,

187
00:06:32,833 --> 00:06:35,324
but anything divided by itself is one.

188
00:06:35,513 --> 00:06:37,664
So ten to the zero is one.

189
00:06:37,928 --> 00:06:38,728
Keep going.

190
00:06:38,909 --> 00:06:41,324
Ten squared over ten to the fifth is ten

191
00:06:41,399 --> 00:06:43,588
to the negative three, and writing it out

192
00:06:43,664 --> 00:06:46,154
leaves three tens on the bottom: one over

193
00:06:46,230 --> 00:06:47,030
a thousand.

194
00:06:47,286 --> 00:06:49,550
So a negative exponent doesn't make a number

195
00:06:49,626 --> 00:06:50,361
negative.

196
00:06:50,381 --> 00:06:51,739
It makes it small.

197
00:06:51,815 --> 00:06:54,079
Ten to the negative three is point zero

198
00:06:54,230 --> 00:06:55,437
zero one.

199
00:06:55,513 --> 00:06:56,796
There's a trap, though.

200
00:06:56,871 --> 00:06:59,211
These rules are for multiplying and dividing,

201
00:06:59,286 --> 00:07:00,418
not adding.

202
00:07:00,494 --> 00:07:02,758
Ten to the fourth plus ten to the fourth

203
00:07:02,871 --> 00:07:04,947
is ten thousand plus ten thousand,

204
00:07:05,022 --> 00:07:06,532
which is twenty thousand.

205
00:07:06,607 --> 00:07:08,645
That's two times ten to the fourth,

206
00:07:08,720 --> 00:07:10,305
not ten to the eighth.

207
00:07:10,381 --> 00:07:13,098
Maya already has a rough idea of the answer:

208
00:07:13,173 --> 00:07:16,267
around a one followed by twenty two zeros.

209
00:07:16,343 --> 00:07:18,758
Theo starts writing zeros and runs out of

210
00:07:18,909 --> 00:07:19,568
room.

211
00:07:19,588 --> 00:07:21,475
So she shows him the shortcut.

212
00:07:21,588 --> 00:07:23,890
A billion is ten to the ninth.

213
00:07:23,966 --> 00:07:25,249
A billion billion?

214
00:07:25,324 --> 00:07:27,513
That's ten to the ninth times ten to the

215
00:07:27,550 --> 00:07:28,323
ninth.

216
00:07:28,343 --> 00:07:30,381
Add the exponents, and you get ten to the

217
00:07:30,494 --> 00:07:31,362
eighteenth.

218
00:07:31,437 --> 00:07:33,550
Then ten thousand of those.

219
00:07:33,626 --> 00:07:35,664
Ten thousand is ten to the fourth,

220
00:07:35,815 --> 00:07:38,003
so add four, and you're at ten to the twenty

221
00:07:38,192 --> 00:07:38,964
second.

222
00:07:38,984 --> 00:07:41,475
Then he asks how small one water molecule

223
00:07:41,550 --> 00:07:42,210
is.

224
00:07:42,230 --> 00:07:44,720
It's roughly three times ten to the negative

225
00:07:44,758 --> 00:07:46,542
tenth of a meter across.

226
00:07:46,607 --> 00:07:49,060
Three ten billionths of a meter.

227
00:07:49,098 --> 00:07:50,871
Line up ten million of them,

228
00:07:50,909 --> 00:07:53,173
ten to the seventh, and the exponents add

229
00:07:53,249 --> 00:07:55,588
to ten to the negative third: about three

230
00:07:55,664 --> 00:07:56,607
millimeters.

231
00:07:56,645 --> 00:07:59,324
Still less than half the width of that drop.

232
00:07:59,362 --> 00:08:00,947
Your turn, two quick ones.

233
00:08:01,022 --> 00:08:03,588
First, ten to the sixth times ten to the

234
00:08:03,739 --> 00:08:06,154
negative ninth, all divided by ten to the

235
00:08:06,230 --> 00:08:07,211
negative two.

236
00:08:07,249 --> 00:08:08,418
It's on screen.

237
00:08:08,607 --> 00:08:11,437
Second, ten cubed plus ten cubed.

238
00:08:17,913 --> 00:08:20,630
On top, six plus negative nine is negative

239
00:08:20,781 --> 00:08:23,196
three, so ten to the negative three.

240
00:08:23,309 --> 00:08:25,988
Then divide: negative three minus negative

241
00:08:26,101 --> 00:08:26,723
two.

242
00:08:26,743 --> 00:08:28,403
Subtracting a negative adds,

243
00:08:28,554 --> 00:08:30,064
so that's negative one.

244
00:08:30,177 --> 00:08:32,177
Ten to the negative one, which is point

245
00:08:32,252 --> 00:08:32,912
one.

246
00:08:32,932 --> 00:08:35,724
And ten cubed plus ten cubed is a thousand

247
00:08:35,799 --> 00:08:37,837
plus a thousand, two thousand.

248
00:08:37,913 --> 00:08:40,026
That's two times ten cubed,

249
00:08:40,343 --> 00:08:41,762
not ten to the sixth.

250
00:08:45,262 --> 00:08:47,601
A glass of water has about eight times ten

251
00:08:47,752 --> 00:08:49,564
to the twenty fourth molecules.

252
00:08:50,016 --> 00:08:51,526
Write out twenty four zeros,

253
00:08:51,601 --> 00:08:53,186
and you will miscount them.

254
00:08:53,488 --> 00:08:56,507
Scientific notation tucks them into an exponent,

255
00:08:56,583 --> 00:08:57,790
in two pieces.

256
00:08:57,866 --> 00:09:00,356
First, a number from one up to just under

257
00:09:00,507 --> 00:09:01,091
ten.

258
00:09:01,111 --> 00:09:02,545
That's the coefficient.

259
00:09:02,696 --> 00:09:04,884
Then a power of ten that says how far the

260
00:09:05,073 --> 00:09:06,394
decimal point moved.

261
00:09:06,469 --> 00:09:08,583
To convert a big number, slide the decimal

262
00:09:08,658 --> 00:09:11,073
point left until there's just one digit,

263
00:09:11,149 --> 00:09:12,960
not a zero, in front of it.

264
00:09:13,035 --> 00:09:14,771
The Earth is about a hundred forty nine

265
00:09:14,922 --> 00:09:17,677
point six million kilometers from the Sun.

266
00:09:17,715 --> 00:09:19,677
Slide the point left eight places,

267
00:09:19,715 --> 00:09:22,167
and it's one point four nine six times ten

268
00:09:22,243 --> 00:09:23,375
to the eighth.

269
00:09:23,450 --> 00:09:25,740
So a big number means you moved left,

270
00:09:25,765 --> 00:09:27,651
and the exponent is positive.

271
00:09:27,702 --> 00:09:29,752
A small number goes the other way.

272
00:09:29,790 --> 00:09:32,054
A fine smoke particle is about six hundred

273
00:09:32,130 --> 00:09:34,243
fifty billionths of a meter wide,

274
00:09:34,318 --> 00:09:35,866
written here as a decimal.

275
00:09:35,903 --> 00:09:38,205
Slide the point right, past the six,

276
00:09:38,243 --> 00:09:40,658
which is seven places, and you get six point

277
00:09:40,733 --> 00:09:43,677
five zero times ten to the negative seventh.

278
00:09:43,752 --> 00:09:45,488
Small number, moved right,

279
00:09:45,564 --> 00:09:46,960
negative exponent.

280
00:09:46,998 --> 00:09:49,979
To multiply, handle the two pieces separately.

281
00:09:50,016 --> 00:09:52,696
Take three point zero times ten to the fourth...

282
00:09:52,733 --> 00:09:54,922
and multiply it by two point zero times

283
00:09:55,035 --> 00:09:56,733
ten to the negative sixth.

284
00:09:56,809 --> 00:09:58,998
Multiply the front numbers: three times

285
00:09:59,186 --> 00:10:00,318
two is six.

286
00:10:00,356 --> 00:10:03,073
Then add the exponents: four plus negative

287
00:10:03,299 --> 00:10:04,809
six is negative two.

288
00:10:04,960 --> 00:10:07,224
So it's six point zero times ten to the

289
00:10:07,450 --> 00:10:08,394
negative two.

290
00:10:08,432 --> 00:10:09,337
Dividing?

291
00:10:09,413 --> 00:10:11,224
Divide the front numbers and subtract the

292
00:10:11,337 --> 00:10:12,243
exponents.

293
00:10:12,281 --> 00:10:14,016
Sometimes the front number comes out too

294
00:10:14,092 --> 00:10:17,186
big, like fifty two times ten to the fifth.

295
00:10:17,287 --> 00:10:20,080
Fifty two is five point two times ten,

296
00:10:20,180 --> 00:10:22,066
so shrink the front number by ten,

297
00:10:22,117 --> 00:10:24,583
and raise the exponent by one: five point

298
00:10:24,658 --> 00:10:26,658
two times ten to the sixth.

299
00:10:26,696 --> 00:10:28,620
Same number, proper form.

300
00:10:28,658 --> 00:10:32,205
On a calculator, the E E or E X P key already

301
00:10:32,394 --> 00:10:34,469
means times ten to the power of.

302
00:10:34,620 --> 00:10:36,658
So for three point two times ten to the

303
00:10:36,847 --> 00:10:39,149
negative fifth, type three point two,

304
00:10:39,224 --> 00:10:41,715
E E, then negative five with the negative

305
00:10:41,790 --> 00:10:43,627
key, not the minus key.

306
00:10:43,752 --> 00:10:45,740
Don't type times ten as well,

307
00:10:45,765 --> 00:10:48,620
or your answer comes out ten times too big.

308
00:10:48,658 --> 00:10:50,960
Now Maya can finally count.

309
00:10:50,998 --> 00:10:53,463
Chemists know what one water molecule weighs,

310
00:10:53,488 --> 00:10:55,752
so they know a gram holds about three point

311
00:10:55,903 --> 00:10:58,092
three four times ten to the twenty second

312
00:10:58,167 --> 00:10:58,827
of them.

313
00:10:58,847 --> 00:11:00,620
The moles video shows how.

314
00:11:00,658 --> 00:11:02,054
She builds the chain.

315
00:11:02,092 --> 00:11:04,092
Point three milliliters per drop...

316
00:11:04,130 --> 00:11:05,991
then one gram per milliliter...

317
00:11:06,042 --> 00:11:08,658
and then, per gram, three point three four

318
00:11:08,847 --> 00:11:11,337
times ten to the twenty second molecules.

319
00:11:11,375 --> 00:11:13,614
Milliliters cancel, grams cancel,

320
00:11:13,665 --> 00:11:15,564
and she's left with molecules.

321
00:11:15,601 --> 00:11:18,243
Point three is three times ten to the negative

322
00:11:18,318 --> 00:11:18,676
one.

323
00:11:18,696 --> 00:11:21,287
Three times three point three four is ten

324
00:11:21,337 --> 00:11:24,356
point zero two, and negative one plus twenty

325
00:11:24,507 --> 00:11:26,243
two is twenty one.

326
00:11:26,318 --> 00:11:28,733
So that's ten point zero two times ten to

327
00:11:28,809 --> 00:11:30,243
the twenty first.

328
00:11:30,356 --> 00:11:32,356
But ten is too big for the front,

329
00:11:32,432 --> 00:11:35,299
so she fixes it: one point zero zero two

330
00:11:35,375 --> 00:11:37,161
times ten to the twenty second.

331
00:11:37,488 --> 00:11:40,130
That's about ten thousand billion billion

332
00:11:40,205 --> 00:11:41,337
molecules...

333
00:11:41,450 --> 00:11:42,318
in one drop.

334
00:11:42,998 --> 00:11:43,798
Your turn.

335
00:11:43,903 --> 00:11:46,318
Multiply four point zero times ten to the

336
00:11:46,356 --> 00:11:48,884
fifth by six point zero times ten to the

337
00:11:49,035 --> 00:11:50,998
negative eighth, and give the answer in

338
00:11:51,186 --> 00:11:53,035
proper scientific notation.

339
00:11:59,888 --> 00:12:02,001
Four times six is twenty four,

340
00:12:02,115 --> 00:12:04,266
and five plus negative eight is negative

341
00:12:04,341 --> 00:12:05,076
three.

342
00:12:05,096 --> 00:12:07,511
So it's twenty four times ten to the negative

343
00:12:07,586 --> 00:12:08,386
third.

344
00:12:08,416 --> 00:12:10,756
But twenty four is too big for the front.

345
00:12:10,983 --> 00:12:13,398
Make it two point four, and raise the exponent

346
00:12:13,473 --> 00:12:16,039
by one: two point four times ten to the

347
00:12:16,115 --> 00:12:16,970
negative second.

348
00:12:17,360 --> 00:12:19,435
A lot of people stop at twenty four times

349
00:12:19,511 --> 00:12:20,907
ten to the negative third.

350
00:12:21,096 --> 00:12:23,435
The value's right, but it isn't scientific

351
00:12:23,737 --> 00:12:24,537
notation.

352
00:12:27,841 --> 00:12:29,199
So, a quick recap.

353
00:12:29,652 --> 00:12:31,539
Keep the unit on every number,

354
00:12:31,615 --> 00:12:33,803
and a conversion factor is just one,

355
00:12:33,879 --> 00:12:35,652
with the unit you have on the bottom.

356
00:12:35,916 --> 00:12:37,313
Multiplying powers of ten?

357
00:12:37,501 --> 00:12:38,483
Add the exponents.

358
00:12:38,784 --> 00:12:39,519
Dividing?

359
00:12:39,539 --> 00:12:40,369
Subtract.

360
00:12:40,596 --> 00:12:42,784
And a negative exponent means small,

361
00:12:42,860 --> 00:12:43,690
not negative.

362
00:12:44,520 --> 00:12:46,633
Scientific notation is a number from one

363
00:12:46,784 --> 00:12:48,558
up to, but not including,

364
00:12:48,747 --> 00:12:50,483
ten, times a power of ten.

365
00:12:53,983 --> 00:12:56,398
Theo whispers the number to himself and

366
00:12:56,624 --> 00:12:57,832
finally heads to bed.

367
00:12:58,133 --> 00:13:00,473
Maya reaches up and turns the faucet handle

368
00:13:00,624 --> 00:13:02,737
hard, and the dripping stops.

369
00:13:03,039 --> 00:13:04,850
The notes and practice for this topic are

370
00:13:04,926 --> 00:13:06,737
free on levlprep.com.

371
00:13:07,266 --> 00:13:10,549
Next time: Maya's cup said thirty milliliters.

372
00:13:10,737 --> 00:13:12,699
Is that one digit she can trust,

373
00:13:12,775 --> 00:13:13,575
or two?

374
00:13:13,862 --> 00:13:15,945
And how many should her answer keep?

375
00:13:16,020 --> 00:13:17,907
That's significant figures.

